WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.105
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:09.861
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.161 --> 00:00:17.910
Unit twenty-four: Data Journalism. Today you will report proportions precisely with twice as… as and three times as many as.

4
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.781
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:25.338
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:27.486
Data journalism.

7
00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:32.840
Data journalism means reporting that uses numbers and datasets to tell a story.

8
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.831
Data journalism revealed the real cost of the project.

9
00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:40.023
Your turn. Say: Data journalism.

10
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:44.170
Data journalism.

11
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:45.802
Dataset.

12
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:49.350
Dataset means a collection of related data.

13
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.089
The dataset covers ten years.

14
00:00:52.683 --> 00:00:55.823
Your turn. Say: Dataset.

15
00:00:58.667 --> 00:00:59.679
Dataset.

16
00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:01.151
Chart.

17
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.403
Chart means a picture that shows information in a clear way.

18
00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:08.144
The chart compares two cities.

19
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.752
Your turn. Say: Chart.

20
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.497
Chart.

21
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.507
Analysis.

22
00:01:17.517 --> 00:01:21.107
Analysis means careful examination to understand something.

23
00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:23.693
The analysis took three weeks.

24
00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.889
Your turn. Say: Analysis.

25
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:31.094
Analysis.

26
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:32.642
Trend.

27
00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:36.973
Trend means a general direction in which something is changing.

28
00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:39.583
The trend is clearly upward.

29
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:42.534
Your turn. Say: Trend.

30
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.169
Trend.

31
00:01:46.917 --> 00:01:48.208
Transparency.

32
00:01:49.217 --> 00:01:52.505
Transparency means being open about sources and methods.

33
00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:56.022
Transparency lets readers check the numbers.

34
00:01:56.617 --> 00:01:59.466
Your turn. Say: Transparency.

35
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.557
Transparency.

36
00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:05.573
Visualisation.

37
00:02:06.583 --> 00:02:10.545
Visualisation means turning data into pictures such as maps and graphs.

38
00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:13.753
The visualisation made the problem obvious.

39
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.362
Your turn. Say: Visualisation.

40
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.433
Visualisation.

41
00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:23.208
Evidence.

42
00:02:24.217 --> 00:02:27.667
Evidence means facts or information that show something is true.

43
00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:30.155
The numbers are strong evidence.

44
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.240
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

45
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.113
Evidence.

46
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.038
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.883 --> 00:02:48.984
Visal, a story says 'Rents are twice as high as they were in 2010.' How is that comparison built?

48
00:02:50.083 --> 00:03:03.895
Multiplier + as + adjective / adverb + as. Twice as high as; three times as fast as; half as expensive as. The first as is an adverb, the second introduces the comparison.

49
00:03:04.983 --> 00:03:06.286
And with nouns?

50
00:03:07.383 --> 00:03:20.647
Use as many as with countable nouns and as much as with uncountable ones: The city has three times as many cyclists as in 2015. Spending is half as much as the average.

51
00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.239
What is the common error?

52
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:36.142
Using than: not 'twice higher than'. Say 'twice as high as' or 'twice the height of'. Check that the comparison is fair: compare rents with rents, not rents with incomes.

53
00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.951
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.817 --> 00:03:44.498
Grammar focus: Proportions: twice and three times and half as… as.

55
00:03:44.798 --> 00:03:45.950
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.857
Rents are twice as high as they were in 2010.

57
00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:55.083
The city has three times as many cyclists as it had in 2015.

58
00:03:55.983 --> 00:03:58.726
Spending is half as much as the national average.

59
00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:09.648
Multiplier + as + adjective + as: twice as high as. With nouns: as many as (countable), as much as (uncountable). Never use than with an as… as pattern.

60
00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:14.197
Be careful! Don't say: Rents are twice higher than before.

61
00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:19.178
Say: Rents are twice as high as before.

62
00:04:19.478 --> 00:04:22.186
Use twice as + adjective + as.

63
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.792
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:27.868
Numbers with Meaning.

65
00:04:28.168 --> 00:04:32.687
Data journalism starts with a dataset and a clear question that the numbers might answer.

66
00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.765
A good analysis finds a trend, such as rents rising twice as fast as wages.

67
00:04:39.767 --> 00:04:43.728
A chart can show that one district has three times as many schools as another.

68
00:04:44.733 --> 00:04:49.751
Transparency means publishing the sources and methods so that readers can check the evidence.

69
00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:55.820
Careful visualisation avoids tricks, because a misleading chart is worse than no chart.

70
00:04:56.933 --> 00:05:00.865
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.717 --> 00:05:04.390
The new bridge is twice as long blank the old one.

72
00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.867
A, as. B, than. C, like.

73
00:05:11.433 --> 00:05:13.061
The answer is A: as.

74
00:05:14.217 --> 00:05:17.609
A general direction in which something is changing is a blank.

75
00:05:17.909 --> 00:05:21.261
A, trend. B, chart. C, dataset.

76
00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.579
The answer is A: trend.

77
00:05:27.717 --> 00:05:29.298
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:29.598 --> 00:05:37.396
A, She has three times as many books as me.. B, She has three times as many books as I do.. C, She has three times many as books..

79
00:05:40.933 --> 00:05:44.930
The answer is B: She has three times as many books as I do..

80
00:05:46.083 --> 00:05:48.756
Being open about sources and methods is blank.

81
00:05:49.056 --> 00:05:53.847
A, analysis. B, transparency. C, visualisation.

82
00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.771
The answer is B: transparency.

83
00:06:00.917 --> 00:06:03.973
According to the reading, what is worse than no chart?

84
00:06:04.273 --> 00:06:09.459
A, a misleading chart. B, a long chart. C, a colourful chart.

85
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.754
The answer is A: a misleading chart.

86
00:06:16.967 --> 00:06:20.014
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:23.151
How can data make a news story stronger?

88
00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.439
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:33.739 --> 00:06:58.290
Data can make a news story stronger by giving clear evidence instead of opinion. A chart or map can show a trend that words alone cannot, and exact numbers let readers compare, for example, one region that has twice as many cases as another. When the writer is transparent about the dataset and the method, readers trust the story more.

90
00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:00.311
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:01.217 --> 00:07:07.224
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and proportions: twice and three times and half as… as.

92
00:07:07.524 --> 00:07:12.023
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

93
00:07:12.323 --> 00:07:15.300
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.135
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

